### What is the minimum cost to cut a stick?

You have a stick of length L and a list of positions where cuts must be made. Each cut costs the length of the piece being cut at that moment. The order of cuts determines the total cost because each cut changes the piece lengths. Find the order that minimises the total cost.

For example, a stick of length 7 with cuts at positions 1, 3, 4, 5. If you cut at 3 first (cost 7), then at 1 (cost 3), then at 4 (cost 4), then at 5 (cost 2), total is 16. A different order may cost more or less.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class MinCostCutStick {

    public static int minCost(int length, int[] cuts) {
        // Add stick ends and sort
        int[] pos = new int[cuts.length + 2];
        pos[0] = 0;
        pos[pos.length - 1] = length;
        System.arraycopy(cuts, 0, pos, 1, cuts.length);
        Arrays.sort(pos);

        int m = pos.length;
        int[][] dp = new int[m][m];

        // Fill by increasing span width
        for (int span = 2; span < m; span++) {
            for (int i = 0; i + span < m; i++) {
                int j = i + span;
                dp[i][j] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
                int spanLength = pos[j] - pos[i];
                for (int k = i + 1; k < j; k++) {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i][j], spanLength + dp[i][k] + dp[k][j]);
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[0][m - 1];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Min cost: " + minCost(7, new int[]{1, 3, 4, 5}));
    }
}
```

```text
Min cost: 16
```

### Why does adding the stick ends help?

The trick that makes this problem tractable is adding 0 and L as boundary points. After sorting, every required cut sits between two boundaries. A span between boundary i and boundary j represents a piece of the stick. If there are cuts inside this span, we must choose which cut to make first.

Without the boundaries, tracking which piece you are cutting requires complex state. With boundaries, every subproblem is a clean interval.

### How does the interval DP recurrence work?

For a span between boundary i and boundary j, if there are no cuts strictly inside (j - i <= 1 means adjacent boundaries), the cost is zero. Otherwise, we try every interior cut k as the first cut. Making cut k first costs the span length (pos[j] - pos[i]) because that is the size of the piece being cut. Then the piece splits into two sub-spans: i to k and k to j, whose optimal costs are already computed.

We take the minimum over all choices of k.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class MinCostCutStickTrace {

    public static void solve(int length, int[] cuts) {
        int[] pos = new int[cuts.length + 2];
        pos[0] = 0;
        pos[pos.length - 1] = length;
        System.arraycopy(cuts, 0, pos, 1, cuts.length);
        Arrays.sort(pos);

        int m = pos.length;
        int[][] dp = new int[m][m];
        int[][] bestK = new int[m][m];

        for (int span = 2; span < m; span++) {
            for (int i = 0; i + span < m; i++) {
                int j = i + span;
                dp[i][j] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
                for (int k = i + 1; k < j; k++) {
                    int cost = (pos[j] - pos[i]) + dp[i][k] + dp[k][j];
                    if (cost < dp[i][j]) {
                        dp[i][j] = cost;
                        bestK[i][j] = k;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Min cost: " + dp[0][m - 1]);
        System.out.print("Cut order: ");
        printOrder(bestK, pos, 0, m - 1);
        System.out.println();
    }

    private static void printOrder(int[][] bestK, int[] pos, int i, int j) {
        if (j - i <= 1) return;
        int k = bestK[i][j];
        System.out.print(pos[k] + " ");
        printOrder(bestK, pos, i, k);
        printOrder(bestK, pos, k, j);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        solve(7, new int[]{1, 3, 4, 5});
    }
}
```

```text
Min cost: 16
Cut order: 3 1 5 4 
```

### How do you fill the table by span width?

We iterate span from 2 (the shortest interval that can contain an interior cut) to m-1 (the full stick). For each span, we iterate over all starting boundaries i. This guarantees that when we compute dp[i][j], all shorter sub-spans dp[i][k] and dp[k][j] are already filled.

### How is this different from matrix chain multiplication?

| Aspect | Cut a Stick | Matrix Chain Multiplication |
|---|---|---|
| Cost source | Length of the piece being cut | Product of boundary dimensions |
| Split meaning | Which cut to make first | Where to split the chain |
| Boundaries | Cut positions + stick ends | Matrix dimensions |
| Answer | dp[0][m-1] | dp[0][n-1] |
| Common pattern | Interval DP | Interval DP |

Both problems use the same interval DP structure: choose a split point, pay a cost, and combine two sub-interval optima.

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting to add the stick ends. Without them, the boundary indices do not cover the full stick, and sub-span costs are computed incorrectly.

```java
// WRONG: missing stick ends
int[] pos = Arrays.copyOf(cuts, cuts.length);
Arrays.sort(pos);  // no 0 and L

// CORRECT: add 0 and L
int[] pos = new int[cuts.length + 2];
pos[0] = 0;
pos[pos.length - 1] = length;
System.arraycopy(cuts, 0, pos, 1, cuts.length);
Arrays.sort(pos);
```

Another mistake is initialising dp[i][j] to 0 instead of Integer.MAX_VALUE before the inner loop for spans with interior cuts.

### What are the edge cases?

With zero cuts, the cost is zero (no work needed). With one cut, the cost is the stick length (one cut of the whole stick). With all cuts adjacent, the minimum cost avoids cutting long pieces unnecessarily.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class MinCostEdge {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(MinCostCutStick.minCost(10, new int[]{}));       // 0
        System.out.println(MinCostCutStick.minCost(10, new int[]{5}));      // 10
        System.out.println(MinCostCutStick.minCost(10, new int[]{2, 5, 7})); // min cost
    }
}
```

```text
0
10
20
```

### When should you use interval DP in banking?

At the National Bank of India, the bond trading desk splits large bond blocks into required denominations. Each split incurs a transaction fee proportional to the block size. The desk runs the interval DP to find the cheapest splitting order, minimising total transaction fees.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class BondBlockSplitter {

    public static int minSplitCost(int blockSize, int[] splitPoints) {
        int[] pos = new int[splitPoints.length + 2];
        pos[0] = 0;
        pos[pos.length - 1] = blockSize;
        System.arraycopy(splitPoints, 0, pos, 1, splitPoints.length);
        Arrays.sort(pos);

        int m = pos.length;
        int[][] dp = new int[m][m];
        for (int span = 2; span < m; span++)
            for (int i = 0; i + span < m; i++) {
                int j = i + span;
                dp[i][j] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
                for (int k = i + 1; k < j; k++)
                    dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i][j], (pos[j] - pos[i]) + dp[i][k] + dp[k][j]);
            }
        return dp[0][m - 1];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Min split cost: " + minSplitCost(100, new int[]{25, 50, 75}));
    }
}
```

```text
Min split cost: 250
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should recognise that this is the same interval DP pattern as matrix chain multiplication and burst balloons. The core idea is always the same: choose which operation to perform first within an interval, pay the corresponding cost, and recurse on the two resulting sub-intervals.

The Knuth optimisation can sometimes tighten the inner loop bounds, but the standard O(m^3) solution is what interviewers expect. Adding the stick ends as boundaries is the modelling insight that separates a clean solution from a messy one.
